00:00:03.469 --> 00:00:04.760
KIRILL TROPIN: Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:05.989
Thank you for coming in.

00:00:05.989 --> 00:00:07.030
My name is Kirill Tropin.

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:09.427
I'm product manager on Google
Cloud Storage and today

00:00:09.427 --> 00:00:11.260
we'll talk about selecting
the right storage

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.050
class for your
workload or whatever

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.610
it might be, either content
storage and delivery, big data

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.040
analytics, or long tail
content that you may not

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.270
be using as much.

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:21.910
I'll have quite a few
speakers joining me on stage

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:23.170
later on in this presentation.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.970
I'll introduce them as we go.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.910
But let's start with Google
Cloud Storage overall.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.600
So GCS, or Google Cloud Storage,
is highly available, durable,

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:33.280
and simple object storage.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.100
We see a lot of success in
GCS across different storage

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.040
classes, big data analytics,
content storage and delivery,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.630
and cold storage as well.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.720
And GCS is built in exactly
the same infrastructure

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.630
as we use internally at Google
for different objects storage.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:48.580
And chances are, if
you have not used GCS

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.520
before, probably you've used
exactly the same technology

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.980
for any other Google products.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.530
And, as I said, exactly
same infrastructures,

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.110
exactly the same engineers
maintaining and building

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:02.410
that infrastructure, same data
centers, same disk, same racks,

00:01:02.410 --> 00:01:05.330
and same network.

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:08.882
So we see quite a lot of
success across those scenarios.

00:01:08.882 --> 00:01:10.840
And let me walk you
through couple examples how

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.670
customers are using GCS today.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:14.920
So starting from left to
right, with content storage

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.930
and delivery, we see
quite a few customers

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.530
using GCS for serving video,
serving music, serving images,

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:23.350
serving any general multimedia
or just serving websites

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:26.400
and static content through GCS.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.695
Then on big data side, if
you have a lot of data,

00:01:28.695 --> 00:01:30.820
If you have petabyte scale,
hundred petabyte scale,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.560
or even exabyte scale, you need
to keep your data somewhere.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.290
And GCS is the place to
keep your exabyte scale data

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.120
and then run compute
jobs on top of that.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.800
So I have quite a
few customers who

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.740
run big data analytics that run
their custom pipelines, custom

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:46.007
workloads.

00:01:46.007 --> 00:01:47.590
They do different
genomics processing,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:50.620
different e-commerce analytics,
IoT, different manufacturing

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.010
modeling, and you see quite
a few of those examples here.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.680
And then, if you look at
those customers, many of them

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:58.760
have hot data and
working data sets

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.220
that they are
processing regularly,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.594
but then they also
have some data

00:02:01.594 --> 00:02:04.559
that they want to archive
or backup for long term.

00:02:04.559 --> 00:02:06.100
And pretty much
every single customer

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.141
will have some sort of
infrequently accessed data

00:02:08.141 --> 00:02:09.910
or some sort of cold data.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.860
Other than that, we
also have customers

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.054
that use GCS purely
for storing cold data

00:02:14.054 --> 00:02:14.970
and long-term archive.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.630
We also have quite
a few partners

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.600
that use GCS to help
ingest data from on-prem

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.010
or help archive different
enterprise data in GCS.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.110
So GCS today has four
main storage classes

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.640
and I'll look in detail
across all of them

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.860
throughout this presentation.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.020
So starting from left
to right, so left,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.510
the hardest one is
Multi-regional storage.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.610
It's built for
storage and delivery.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.680
It's geo redundant so
it's perfect for business

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:41.770
continuating.

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.950
If a single region
in GCS goes down

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.505
or something happens
to that region,

00:02:45.505 --> 00:02:46.880
you don't need to
worry about it.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.963
We'll keep serving your
requests because your data

00:02:48.963 --> 00:02:50.880
is geo redundant across
two or more regions

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.500
within a multi-regional
location.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.370
And we see customers use that
storage for streaming videos,

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.210
serving images and website
content and documents.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.730
Second class is
regional storage class.

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.730
It's built for data analytics
within a single region.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.980
If you are running some
computer workloads,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.020
you want to have your
data stored right next

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.852
to your computer workloads.

00:03:11.852 --> 00:03:13.560
You want to have lowest
latency possible,

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.990
high throughput possible,
and regional storage class

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.240
is the right place
for storing data

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.640
for different analytics
workloads or general computer

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.690
workloads.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.200
And both of those storage
classes are built for hard data

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.480
and they provide highest
level of availability

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.610
across our four storage classes.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.230
Then moving to the
right, you will

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.740
see two storage classes that
are built for cold or cool data.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.200
So that's Nearline, which
is built for data access

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.430
less than once a month.

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.700
And then its Coldline data
that's accessed less than once

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.200
a year on average.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.390
And we see customers use
those storage classes

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:48.630
for serving long tail content
or for disaster recovery

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.130
or for archive.

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:52.080
Beautiful part about
those two storage classes

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.390
is they have exactly the
same API as any other storage

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.230
class in GCS.

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.630
So it's very easy for you to
integrate with those storage

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:58.130
classes.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.350
Once you start using GCS, either
it's hot data or cold data,

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.030
you can use any
other storage class

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.860
because its exactly
the same API.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.420
And for all of our
storage classes,

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.230
we provide online latency.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.110
So that means you
don't need to engineer

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.210
around data
retrievals or anything

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:14.220
like that, because
all of the latency

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.149
is consistent across
storage classes.

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.610
So let's go into detail into
content storage and delivery.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:21.640
I'll start with our
Multi-regional storage.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.950
And then I'll invite product
manager from Firebase

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.460
to talk more about
Firebase and how

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:28.390
it's helpful for content
storage and delivery.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:32.379
So our Multi-regional storage
is best for content serving.

00:04:32.379 --> 00:04:34.670
As I said before, it's geo
redundant across two or more

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:35.690
regions.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:39.350
And we provide very simple
API and very simple pricing.

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:42.520
So it's exactly the same
API as for regional storage.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:46.810
Just provide location name, in
this case it's US, EU, or Asia.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:49.120
And then you create a bucket
and start uploading data.

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:51.440
You don't need to worry about
your primary or secondary

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.500
regions, we'll take
care of all of that.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.960
It's a very simple API to use.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.820
And because data
is geo redundant,

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.912
we'll take care of
different failovers.

00:04:59.912 --> 00:05:01.870
And it's very good for
your business continuity

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:03.536
as well, because you
don't need to worry

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:07.200
about some region going
down inside of GCS.

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.540
So as I said, we have three
Multi-regional locations today.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.071
So we have a lot of different
regions in Google Cloud

00:05:13.071 --> 00:05:13.570
platform.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:15.940
And we're building, pretty
much a new region every month

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:16.930
or every two months.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.096
So right now, we have three
Multi-regional locations

00:05:19.096 --> 00:05:21.080
that unite multiple
regions inside of them.

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.990
So one of them is US,
the other one is EU,

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:24.880
and third one is Asia.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.627
And customers that
want to serve content

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:29.710
either within those locations
or around the world,

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:34.810
they could use one of
those storage locations.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:37.420
So let me talk a little bit
more about that simple API

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.020
for Multi-regional
storage classes.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:41.680
So here's an example of creating
a Multi-regional storage

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:44.490
class with a common line
utilities called gsutil.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.280
And I provide a type of storage
class, which is Multi-regional.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:49.960
I provide location, US.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.280
And then I just name my bucket.

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.290
Literally, there is nothing
else you need to provide.

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:54.748
We'll take care of
everything else.

00:05:54.748 --> 00:05:56.679
We'll take care of where
to store your data,

00:05:56.679 --> 00:05:58.220
and you don't need
to worry about it.

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.310
And when you retrieve
your data, again, you

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:01.810
don't need to specify
any locations.

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:05.490
Just provide your bucket
name, domain name, and then

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:06.740
the name of your file.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:07.240
That's it.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.823
You don't need to
worry about routing.

00:06:08.823 --> 00:06:10.990
We'll take care of routing
within our network.

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:14.710
You don't need to worry if
it's east, west, Europe, US.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:18.164
We'll take care of all of that
routing on our Google network.

00:06:18.164 --> 00:06:20.080
Multi-regional storage
class is also optimized

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:22.840
for end user latency, because
there are multiple layers

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.730
of caching that we have in GCS.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:27.430
So even though you might be
storing data in Multi-regional

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.440
US, if you have customers
that are accessing

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:32.147
the data from Europe, we'll
cache the data in Europe

00:06:32.147 --> 00:06:32.980
in our data centers.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.000
Similarly, in Asia Pacific,
we'll cache in our data centers

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.210
in Asia Pacific.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:41.760
And on top of that, if you
set your objects to be public,

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.867
we'll also cache those
objects at the edge

00:06:43.867 --> 00:06:44.950
in our points of presence.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.410
We have around 100 points of
presence around the world.

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:49.235
And we'll leverage the
same infrastructure

00:06:49.235 --> 00:06:51.100
as for other internal
Google products

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:52.480
or, for instance, YouTube.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.720
And we'll cache the data in
those points of presence.

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:58.720
So your consumers in large
cities around the world

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.240
will have extremely low latency,
because data will actually

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.573
be served from location right
next to them in that city.

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:07.870
In terms of price,
we charge very simple

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:11.500
2.6 cents per gigabyte
month for your data

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:12.870
in Multi-regional storage class.

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:15.160
And we'll take care of all
their applications underneath.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:17.701
You don't need to worry about
paying for network to replicate

00:07:17.701 --> 00:07:19.280
your data across data centers.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:22.480
You don't need to worry about
secondary copy or tertiary

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:23.200
copy.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:25.570
Everything is included
in that simple 2.6

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:27.550
cents per gigabyte month price.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.416
We'll also charge
for read operations

00:07:29.416 --> 00:07:30.290
and write operations.

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:34.890
And we've recently decreased
the price by 50% and 60%.

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.790
We decreased it half a year ago.

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:40.390
And we have very
competitive price on storage

00:07:40.390 --> 00:07:42.460
and the operations there.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:46.060
In terms of availability,
typically, the storage class

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:47.720
runs at four 9's
availability, and we

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:49.390
provide 3 and 1/2 9's SLA.

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:52.660
So if for some reason
it drops, within a given

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:54.880
month below 3 and 1/2
9's, we'll pay you back

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:56.500
for using that storage
class depending

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.510
on how available it was.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.180
And it's also designed
for 11 9's durability.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.835
All of our storage
classes are durable.

00:08:02.835 --> 00:08:04.960
So you don't need to worry
about some storage class

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:06.376
being less durable
than the other.

00:08:06.376 --> 00:08:10.500
We provide the same standard
durability across all of them.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.960
Turns out, a lot
of data is actually

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:14.730
being consumed on mobile phones.

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:17.250
So it's extremely
important for our customers

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.850
to be able to build applications
and be able to serve the data

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.620
and consume the data
from a mobile phone.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.911
So I'd like to invite Mike
McDonald, who is the Project

00:08:24.911 --> 00:08:27.240
Manager on Firebase, to talk
about using Google Cloud

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.935
Storage with Firebase APA.

00:08:29.935 --> 00:08:31.450
MIKE MCDONALD: Thanks, Kirill.

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:32.980
How is everyone doing today?

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:33.520
Good?

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:34.510
Awesome.

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:36.669
How many of you have
heard of Firebase?

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:38.120
Couple people.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.750
How many of you
operate mobile apps?

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:44.980
Hopefully, oh come on, you
guys the world is mobile first.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.440
So what Firebase is,
oh I have a clicker.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.180
Firebase is Google's
mobile platform.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:54.970
So Firebase allows you to
develop your applications

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:59.600
on Google Cloud Platform, grow
your user base using our--

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.660
we have push
notifications, we have

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.062
application invites, all
of that kind of stuff.

00:09:04.062 --> 00:09:05.770
And we also have a
monetization platform.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:10.060
So you can earn more money by
integrating with our products.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:11.890
But for those of
you who are building

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.598
mobile apps, how many
of your mobile apps

00:09:13.598 --> 00:09:15.680
are serving some
kind of rich content?

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:20.980
So you're serving media, you're
serving images or videos.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:23.770
All of that, you need to
be able to easily upload

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:26.440
from the user's mobile
device and then transmit

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.390
those around the world.

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.630
Firebase offers really great
mobile support for Google Cloud

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:34.550
Storage through our SDKs.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:36.065
And those SDKs--

00:09:36.065 --> 00:09:37.560
I'm going to go to
the next slide--

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:40.130
are optimized for
mobile content delivery.

00:09:40.130 --> 00:09:44.930
So we handle tricky problems
like really granular security.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:47.960
So if you want your users
to sign-in via Facebook

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.420
and upload a photo,
we can integrate

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.130
with Firebase
authentication, which

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:55.250
does server list sign-on to
Facebook and then lets you,

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:58.760
again, without a server, upload
directly from your device

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.650
to Google Cloud Storage, to any
Google Cloud Storage bucket.

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.600
We also do that on the clients.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.160
We have very robust networking.

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:09.370
So how many of you guys are
enjoying the conference Wi-Fi?

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:10.790
Things are pretty slow.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:13.750
Are you trying to send tweets
and take pictures and share

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:14.620
them with people?

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:16.660
Have you had any uploads fail?

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:18.860
It's because people
aren't doing it robustly.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:23.052
So our networks our SDKs are
going to do resumable uploads.

00:10:23.052 --> 00:10:24.760
And I'll actually show
that, because I've

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:27.835
been having fun doing my demo
earlier over this network.

00:10:27.835 --> 00:10:30.460
And so we're going to make sure
that your uploads and downloads

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.100
get to where they need to
go even if the network is

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:33.880
terrible.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.380
And as Krill has
been talking about,

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.300
it's all built for Google scale.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:39.910
So we support five
terabyte uploads direct

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:42.100
from your mobile phone.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.920
And I actually have a standing
thing, you can tweet at me.

00:10:44.920 --> 00:10:47.410
If someone ever finds out
how to upload five terabytes

00:10:47.410 --> 00:10:50.230
from a mobile phone, let me know
because I have a job for you.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:56.840
So let's switch over to the
demo, because listening to me

00:10:56.840 --> 00:11:00.140
talk about this is
really not exciting.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:01.970
How many people have
programmed an iOS app?

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:04.790
Or does SWIFT code look
at all familiar to people?

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:05.750
Maybe?

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:08.870
Hopefully it's not too crazy.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.709
I have over here an iPhone app.

00:11:11.709 --> 00:11:14.000
And it's going to look really
boring when I start it up

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.890
because there's
nothing going on.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:18.110
But if you click that
little camera button,

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.012
it's going to take us
over to a photo picker.

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:21.470
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.650
going to take a picture from
the phone, upload Google Cloud

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.560
Storage, and then download
it again to the device.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.370
So we're going to do
that all with Firebase.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:36.080
So this is actually about 100
lines of code just as a shell.

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:38.380
But, yes this works.

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:40.150
We are going to--

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:42.810
and ah-ha, I didn't
delete my sample.

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:43.900
There we go.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:47.600
We're going to first
upload the image.

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.400
So we're going to create a
reference to our Cloud storage

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:53.970
bucket.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:59.520
And then, we are going
to upload our image just

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:00.630
using this PUT method.

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:03.840
And so that takes the
image that we clicked,

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:07.080
and it's going to upload
that Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:13.350
We'll also add an observer
to let us know the progress,

00:12:13.350 --> 00:12:17.254
because I was noticing earlier
today it was pretty slow.

00:12:17.254 --> 00:12:18.920
So what this is going
to do is it's just

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.420
going to print out
and let us know

00:12:20.420 --> 00:12:22.250
how quickly things are going.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:24.680
So I will rerun that.

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.560
And to prove to you that
this is going to work,

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.690
the app will open up over here.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.490
We will select a photo.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:36.590
By the way, the
Apple System Photos

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.169
are about 20 megs in size.

00:12:38.169 --> 00:12:38.960
They're pretty big.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:39.710
Oh, and there we go.

00:12:39.710 --> 00:12:41.376
Actually, the internet
was pretty quick.

00:12:41.376 --> 00:12:44.600
So you can see that we
fully uploaded our file.

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:50.830
Let's pop over to the Firebase
console and refresh the page.

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:52.540
And we can go ahead
and see, we now

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:56.440
have a folder that says
"Photos" and we even

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.520
have the photo in there.

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:01.690
That was like one line of
code that uploaded a photo.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:02.190
No?

00:13:02.190 --> 00:13:02.690
No clapping?

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:03.189
OK.

00:13:03.189 --> 00:13:03.780
[APPLAUSE]

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.640
Thank you.

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:08.010
Small applause, that's--
we're not done yet.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.560
So now we're going
to synchronize that.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:12.890
So we're actually using
another Firebase product.

00:13:12.890 --> 00:13:14.310
It's our Realtime Database.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:16.560
And we've already written
that data, basically

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:20.460
the sharable URL for that
location in Cloud Storage.

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:25.720
And now, we're going to download
that image and display it.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:28.230
So all you need to
do there is, you

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:31.110
can take that URL that we
synchronized via our database

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.570
and just say, get the data.

00:13:33.570 --> 00:13:35.940
And they're about 20
megs, so I'm making sure

00:13:35.940 --> 00:13:36.960
that we have enough.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:40.540
And then we simply put
it in our app and reload.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:44.251
We'll go ahead and
start that back up.

00:13:44.251 --> 00:13:46.250
And the nice thing,
actually, about the database

00:13:46.250 --> 00:13:48.680
is it's going to immediately
take the first photo that I

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.080
uploaded last, and it's
downloading it right now.

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.370
And there it is.

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:54.770
So that was actually
pretty quick.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:57.010
But because I like
to live dangerously,

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:59.330
and because I'm a Millennial,
I have another version

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.490
of the app on my phone.

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:05.990
And I'm going to take a
selfie with all of you.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:11.870
And let's see how
long this takes.

00:14:11.870 --> 00:14:13.894
So it's uploading right now.

00:14:13.894 --> 00:14:14.560
And there we go.

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:15.800
Oh, I'm upside down.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:16.820
Sorry about that.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:17.730
But there you go.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:19.970
So that was maybe
10 lines of code,

00:14:19.970 --> 00:14:22.160
including all of the
application logic,

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.110
to upload and download
from Google Cloud

00:14:24.110 --> 00:14:25.990
Storage on an iPhone app.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:26.990
Thank you all very much.

00:14:26.990 --> 00:14:30.330
[APPLAUSE]

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:31.410
And back to Kirill.

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:33.990
KIRILL TROPIN: Thank you, Mike.

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:36.480
So all of that is still backed
by Google Cloud Storage.

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.575
So that means, when you
are leveraging Firebase,

00:14:39.575 --> 00:14:41.700
all of the data will be
transferred by Google Cloud

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:42.367
Storage--

00:14:42.367 --> 00:14:43.950
will be stored on
Google Cloud Storage

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:45.420
and transferred
by Google Network.

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:46.950
So it will be amazingly fast.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:50.770
And it will scale to extremely
large applications that serving

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:52.600
that content around the world.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.326
So let's go back to
big data analytics now.

00:14:55.326 --> 00:14:56.700
So if you have a
lot of data, you

00:14:56.700 --> 00:14:58.920
need a place to store it
before you analyze it.

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:00.990
And Google Cloud
Storage Regional Bucket

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:02.940
is the right place
to store the data.

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:05.610
So data with GCS Regional
is redundant within a given

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:06.330
region.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:09.132
And if you select
Compute Engine and GCS

00:15:09.132 --> 00:15:10.590
Regional in the
same region, you'll

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:13.350
get the lowest latency
possible between those two.

00:15:13.350 --> 00:15:15.922
And it's built for
data access frequently.

00:15:15.922 --> 00:15:17.880
So that means if you have
some application that

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:21.060
process that data lot this is
the best storage class to use.

00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:22.800
Similarly to any
other storage class,

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:25.320
GCS is very deeply integrated
with other products

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:28.350
within Google Cloud
Platform, like Data Procmail,

00:15:28.350 --> 00:15:31.080
Google Compute Engine,
Genomics, even BigQuery.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.250
So you can potentially
store data in GCS

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:37.800
and query it directly out of
BigQuery, [INAUDIBLE] query.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:39.795
And a lot of customers
use this storage class

00:15:39.795 --> 00:15:42.249
to run different pipelines
and analyze their data

00:15:42.249 --> 00:15:44.790
from either of their customer
plans and Google Compute Engine

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:46.170
or by our products.

00:15:46.170 --> 00:15:47.670
And because it's
so well-integrated,

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:49.080
it's very easy to get started--

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:51.840
let's say, when you're
migrating from Hadoop on-prem,

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.550
migrating to Google
Cloud Platform.

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:55.620
So one of our products
in Google Cloud Platform

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:56.890
was Cloud DataProc.

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.610
And it's essentially a managed
Spark and Hadoop service.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.770
And we have a Cloud
Storage connector,

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.369
which is HCFS compliant
as part of the product.

00:16:04.369 --> 00:16:05.910
And that means it's
very easy for you

00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:10.170
to move from Hadoop on-prem
to GCS and DataProc.

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:13.170
So here's the example at the
bottom of changing your code,

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:15.240
essentially just
changing one line.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:16.950
It's how you're
referencing your storage.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:19.366
And everything else, all the
other code will keep working.

00:16:19.366 --> 00:16:22.230
And that Cloud Storage connector
will take care of everything.

00:16:22.230 --> 00:16:25.290
And you don't need to change
any other lines of code.

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:27.860
And that solves a lot of
problems for customers.

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:29.370
Because data is
stored in GCS, you

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:31.860
don't need to worry about
scaling that data over time,

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:34.830
like you might need
to worry on-prem.

00:16:34.830 --> 00:16:37.570
The data is also accessible
outside of your Hadoop

00:16:37.570 --> 00:16:38.070
clusters.

00:16:38.070 --> 00:16:40.403
You can turn it down, and
your data will still be there.

00:16:40.403 --> 00:16:41.520
It's still in GCS.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.010
You can turn up another
cluster and access the data.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.810
So that provides a
lot of simplicity

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:47.864
and a lot of flexibility
for our customers.

00:16:47.864 --> 00:16:49.530
If you want to read
more about DataProc,

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:54.100
you can go to
cloud.google.com/dataproc/.

00:16:54.100 --> 00:16:56.490
Similarly to our
Multi-regional storage,

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:59.670
we charge a very simple price
of $0.02 per gigabyte month.

00:16:59.670 --> 00:17:01.220
We charge also for operations.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.540
And we provide three
9's availability SLA.

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:05.890
So as you see, our SLA for
regional storage class is

00:17:05.890 --> 00:17:07.890
slightly lower than the
one from Multi-regional,

00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:09.349
where we provide 3 and 1/2 9's.

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:11.099
And that's because
regional storage is not

00:17:11.099 --> 00:17:14.220
geo redundant as
Multi-regional storage class.

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:16.294
And it also has
11 9's durability.

00:17:16.294 --> 00:17:19.290
It has instant access,
like anything else in GCS.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:22.079
And we have exactly the
same API that you would

00:17:22.079 --> 00:17:23.661
use across all storage classes.

00:17:23.661 --> 00:17:25.619
So once you start using
regional storage class,

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:28.890
you can easily switch to
using any other storage class.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.650
But enough hearing from me.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:32.880
I'd prefer to hear
from real customer.

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:35.829
So I'd like to invite Fastly,
our dear customer and partner,

00:17:35.829 --> 00:17:37.620
and specifically, Artur
Bergman, their CEO.

00:17:45.706 --> 00:17:46.770
ARTUR BERGMAN: Thank you.

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.085
Thank you.

00:17:48.085 --> 00:17:48.770
This works?

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:49.766
All right.

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.320
Hello, everyone.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:00.420
My name is Artur Bergman.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:02.970
I'm CEO of Fastly.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:06.090
And we are both, as
Kirill said, a customer

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:09.390
and a partner of Google.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:13.080
So what we are is a edge cloud.

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:18.315
So in the old world, you had
a whole bunch of edge devices

00:18:18.315 --> 00:18:20.400
in your own data center.

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.860
And as you're
moving to the cloud,

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:28.410
those old, expensive,
inflexible, unreliable hardware

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:30.930
machines that you had at
the edge of your data center

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:33.660
have no longer anywhere to live.

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:35.850
And so that's where we come in.

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:39.370
So we complement very well
together with central cloud,

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:41.550
so we integrated really
well with Google.

00:18:41.550 --> 00:18:43.920
So we provide
caching, edge compute,

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:47.520
TLFS termination, load
balancing disability,

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:50.900
as a complement to the
storage and computing and et

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.790
cetera that you get
on the other side.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:57.690
So how it works, we have
interconnections with Google

00:18:57.690 --> 00:19:00.140
in quite a few locations
around the world

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:01.530
to the Google network.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:05.190
And then we have our own POPs
all around the world, where

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:08.190
we store your content,
process your content,

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:11.930
defend your content
to end users,

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:15.720
whether those are humans,
or machines, or et cetera,

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:17.460
all around the world.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:21.380
So could also, at some
point, be described as a CDN.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:28.430
One of the really cool things
that we found with Google

00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:30.740
is the cloud functions.

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:33.530
So one of the core
functionality we provide

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:36.680
is that you want the view
that the rest of the world

00:19:36.680 --> 00:19:39.000
has of your content
to be up-to-date.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.210
So we do cache
invalidation, or purging,

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:43.750
in about 150
milliseconds worldwide.

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:47.400
And so here's a very simple
cloud function for Google Cloud

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:50.520
Storage, so that when
you upload or change

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.945
an image or any piece of
data on Google Cloud Storage,

00:19:52.945 --> 00:19:55.220
that cache invalidation
goes out worldwide.

00:19:55.220 --> 00:20:00.000
And the new content is
available from any edge POP,

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.420
completely transparently.

00:20:03.420 --> 00:20:08.990
So we really like working with
partners who are really, really

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:13.940
good and provide really good
performance, and service,

00:20:13.940 --> 00:20:16.260
and reliability
to our customers.

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:19.310
Because at the end
of the day, we're

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:22.520
here to provide fantastic
reliability and performance

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:23.720
to our customers.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:25.190
And if we bring in
partners or work

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:27.780
with partners who don't,
that causes problems.

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:32.660
And so I went and looked
at some performance metrics

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:36.290
that we're collecting
from Google Cloud Storage

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:37.940
versus Amazon S3.

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:40.125
And this is not
a synthetic test.

00:20:40.125 --> 00:20:44.220
This is actually looking
at very, very large data

00:20:44.220 --> 00:20:48.530
sets of content being
fetched from both.

00:20:48.530 --> 00:20:51.470
And the difference
is pretty dramatic.

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:56.270
So the scale on the
lower is different,

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:58.650
but what you're seeing
there is a average Time

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:02.150
To First Byte of three
to five milliseconds

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:05.120
versus 200 milliseconds.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:09.920
And so again, this is
two very large data sets,

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:14.370
where we're fetching cache
misses from S3 or Google Cloud

00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:16.370
Storage.

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:22.280
And in both cases, it's
S3 East and GCS East,

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:24.770
which is one of the
reasons we really

00:21:24.770 --> 00:21:26.660
love working with
Google, because this just

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:29.240
creates a better
experience for everyone--

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:32.690
both engineers, and our
customers, and their end users.

00:21:37.660 --> 00:21:40.300
So at the use cases
we're looking at,

00:21:40.300 --> 00:21:43.430
and we have some pretty
amazing customers together--

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:46.415
Spotify, Vimeo, and
Firebase was earlier--

00:21:49.210 --> 00:21:52.660
obviously, our own caching,
caching closer to user.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:55.240
But also in the big
data analytics side,

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:57.120
we integrate really well.

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.640
And from a security
point of view,

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:04.330
the real time aspects of the
platform and the integration

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:07.060
of Google allows you to do
some pretty amazing stuff.

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:14.140
So use cases are, in
this case, pretty simple,

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:16.860
especially like Vimeo, that's,
obviously, video and images.

00:22:16.860 --> 00:22:20.430
Spotify is, as people can guess,
a tremendous amount of audio.

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.530
But there's a lot
of other cool things

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:23.880
that they do with us as well.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:29.420
We also do real time
log files from the edge,

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:33.290
which is pretty key, because
as you're moving functionality

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:37.610
that you used to have in
your own sphere of control,

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:38.970
you need that--

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:41.600
when you had it in your own data
center and your own control,

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:43.730
you knew what was
going on in real time.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:47.990
And you need that once you move
it out in the cloud as well.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:49.520
And so we have
these real time logs

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:51.290
that update in about a second.

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:54.980
And we can stream those
into Google Cloud Storage

00:22:54.980 --> 00:23:00.005
and then get that into BigQuery
in pretty much real time.

00:23:00.005 --> 00:23:04.780
That's something we use
internally very much.

00:23:04.780 --> 00:23:09.350
So seeing that two-way
integration just

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.760
creates a really nice
experience for engineers,

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:15.620
for developers, and operations.

00:23:15.620 --> 00:23:22.930
So internally, we use
that a lot for analyzing

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:28.840
performance of GCP connections
between end users and us.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:35.050
So we see on the order
of 3 or 4 million TCP

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:37.180
connections per second.

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:39.890
At any given time,
we'll have about 150,

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.100
200 million TCP
connections open.

00:23:42.100 --> 00:23:45.550
And we want to know as
much data about these as

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:49.000
possible to detect problems
on the internet router, around

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:50.660
those problems, et cetera.

00:23:50.660 --> 00:23:52.420
And what we ended up
using is BigQuery.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:54.940
And we put in somewhere
between about 50,000

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:59.320
and 100,000 records per
second into BigQuery.

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:01.510
We also use it for
edge enforcement,

00:24:01.510 --> 00:24:03.540
which I'll go into in a second.

00:24:03.540 --> 00:24:06.800
And then we have customers
who use it in the AdTech space

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.467
or for Beacons that are
collecting data through Fastly.

00:24:09.467 --> 00:24:11.550
And then, instead of having
to run their own data,

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:14.230
like their own servers,
to terminate that request

00:24:14.230 --> 00:24:16.630
and put it into
Google Cloud Storage,

00:24:16.630 --> 00:24:18.460
they're just having
Fastly dump it directly

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:22.357
into Cloud Storage, so
serverless, basically.

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:27.490
We use it for edge
threat analytics.

00:24:27.490 --> 00:24:32.200
So we have a live product,
and we provide edge security

00:24:32.200 --> 00:24:34.230
for our customers.

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:38.400
So we use GCS, and
BigQuery, and edge

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:42.310
logs for that and
basically, very quickly can

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:45.700
pull any kind of
report from the edge

00:24:45.700 --> 00:24:48.760
to see what rules are
hitting, what kind of threats

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:54.410
we're seeing, anomaly
scores, et cetera.

00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:56.720
And we can then expose
that to our customers.

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:59.500
So the total
pipeline of end user

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:02.470
fetching something from
us, we don't have it,

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:03.649
we fetch it from Google.

00:25:03.649 --> 00:25:05.440
If we have it, we give
them, we process it,

00:25:05.440 --> 00:25:10.390
we apply whatever rules and
logic around that we need to.

00:25:10.390 --> 00:25:12.130
And then we log it into Google.

00:25:12.130 --> 00:25:15.030
And then we use the Google
Tools for Analytics.

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:17.830
And so you can then integrate
that with whatever pipelines

00:25:17.830 --> 00:25:22.810
you have for other kind of
analytics, for using Tableau

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:25.530
or something like that, or
one of the security packages.

00:25:25.530 --> 00:25:28.800
It works really well.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:32.980
And you can use the standard
Google Visualizations

00:25:32.980 --> 00:25:38.080
that they have to just create
reports really, really fast.

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:42.190
So the combination really
lets you build a very, very

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:46.900
compelling replacement
to the old combination

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:51.670
of having your own storage and
then a boring CDN out there

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:56.050
that doesn't actually do what
you want to do on either side.

00:25:56.050 --> 00:26:00.160
That combination, that
two-way integration

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:03.550
really allows you to
drive a lot of value.

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:07.160
And the value really
comes from two points.

00:26:07.160 --> 00:26:11.560
One is the customer experience,
your end user experience.

00:26:11.560 --> 00:26:13.840
But I think a very
important point is also

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:18.840
the internal engineering
and product experience.

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:23.140
So it allows your developers, it
allows your product engineers,

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:26.380
your product managers
to just move faster

00:26:26.380 --> 00:26:29.080
and to get new
functionality out,

00:26:29.080 --> 00:26:34.660
because all the APIs, all the
documentation, API functions,

00:26:34.660 --> 00:26:38.060
modules, et cetera, are just
so accessible in real time.

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:40.900
You don't have to go through
this kind of painful process

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:43.180
that you traditionally
had to do to get all

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:44.710
these systems working together.

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:47.920
So you can just
release code faster,

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:50.900
keep your engineers happier,
and keep, by extension, your end

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:52.400
users faster.

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:54.760
So we're big fans of Google.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.820
We use them internally.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:58.780
We have very big
customers together.

00:26:58.780 --> 00:27:04.350
And we would love to have more
of you using both us and Google

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:05.000
together.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:05.708
Thanks, everyone.

00:27:05.708 --> 00:27:09.727
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:10.787 --> 00:27:12.120
KIRILL TROPIN: Thank you, Artur.

00:27:14.770 --> 00:27:18.330
So we've talked a lot about big
data analytics and the content

00:27:18.330 --> 00:27:19.600
serving.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:22.630
But all of those customers they
have some sort of cold data.

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:25.030
So over time, they
collect a lot of data.

00:27:25.030 --> 00:27:27.400
And that data may not
be as commonly used

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:30.130
as it used to be when it
was just first generated.

00:27:30.130 --> 00:27:33.190
And typically, customers will
want to save money on the data.

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:36.490
I'm sure everyone wants to save
money on their infrastructure.

00:27:36.490 --> 00:27:38.600
And if the data is not
being used that often,

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.487
probably you want to
pay less for the data.

00:27:40.487 --> 00:27:42.070
And so I will have
two storage classes

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:44.166
that are designed for
cool and cold data.

00:27:44.166 --> 00:27:45.290
So one of them is Nearline.

00:27:45.290 --> 00:27:46.450
Another one is Coldline.

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:48.280
I'll start with Nearline here.

00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:50.030
So Nearline is a
storage class that

00:27:50.030 --> 00:27:52.215
is designed for
infrequently accessed data.

00:27:52.215 --> 00:27:54.650
It provides the same instant
access speed and the same API

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:55.900
as other storage classes.

00:27:55.900 --> 00:27:57.954
And it's extremely
important for our customers.

00:27:57.954 --> 00:28:00.370
So what that means, if you're
using Multi-regional storage

00:28:00.370 --> 00:28:02.230
class for content
serving and delivery

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:04.139
and then, turns out
not all of your data

00:28:04.139 --> 00:28:06.430
is frequently accessed and
you want to store some of it

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:08.110
in Nearline, well,
it turns out you can

00:28:08.110 --> 00:28:09.425
use all of your integrations.

00:28:09.425 --> 00:28:11.050
You can use our
partnership with Fastly

00:28:11.050 --> 00:28:13.150
to serve the content
around the world

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:15.160
or you can use any
big data tools that we

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:16.637
have at Google Cloud Platform.

00:28:16.637 --> 00:28:18.220
And you don't need
to change anything,

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:20.140
because it's exactly
the same API.

00:28:20.140 --> 00:28:21.760
It's exactly the same latency.

00:28:21.760 --> 00:28:23.980
You don't need to do anything
extra on top of that.

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:27.080
So some typical examples
include storing historical data

00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:29.390
and then maybe analyzing
the data in the future.

00:28:29.390 --> 00:28:32.590
So sometimes you don't know
how the data might end up being

00:28:32.590 --> 00:28:33.800
useful for you in the future.

00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:35.980
So you want to keep it
around and still probably

00:28:35.980 --> 00:28:38.860
have low latency
and some chances

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:40.570
to access the data
in the future.

00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:43.760
Customers also use Nearline
to serve long tail content.

00:28:43.760 --> 00:28:46.076
So if you have a lot of
different multimedia content,

00:28:46.076 --> 00:28:47.950
some of it will not be
as frequently accessed

00:28:47.950 --> 00:28:48.910
as the other ones.

00:28:48.910 --> 00:28:51.460
So you can put your cold content
or your long tail content

00:28:51.460 --> 00:28:53.070
on Nearline.

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:54.670
So for Nearline,
we charge you $0.01

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:56.410
per gigabyte month on storage.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.320
And we also charge for
access of $0.01 per gigabyte

00:28:59.320 --> 00:29:01.540
of data right out of Nearline.

00:29:01.540 --> 00:29:04.460
And if you recall those two
numbers and storage price

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:06.600
for Multi-regional
storage, turns out

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:08.350
that Nearline becomes
the cheapest storage

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.614
class for data that's accessed
less than once a month.

00:29:10.614 --> 00:29:12.280
And that's where that
statement comes in

00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:16.020
that Nearline is built
for data that is accessed

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:16.990
less than once a month.

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:18.250
For Nearline we provide SLA.

00:29:18.250 --> 00:29:20.050
We provide two 9's SLA.

00:29:20.050 --> 00:29:22.900
And it's consistent API
and consistent performance,

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:25.180
like with any other
storage classes.

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:27.850
So if you've been with
us for a couple of years

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:30.160
you might have seen
Nearline two years ago.

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.870
And back then, we had four
second latency on Nearline.

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:34.819
And we've been working
very hard and improving

00:29:34.819 --> 00:29:37.360
performance and reliability of
Nearline and any other storage

00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:38.210
class.

00:29:38.210 --> 00:29:41.794
So over last year we've
increased availability SLA

00:29:41.794 --> 00:29:44.210
on Multi-regional storage class
and also decreased latency

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:46.480
in Nearline all the
way from four seconds

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:49.210
all the way down to instant
access speed with Nearline.

00:29:49.210 --> 00:29:51.130
And we are committed
to continuously improve

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:53.500
our performance and reliability
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:54.875
And those improvements
that we've

00:29:54.875 --> 00:29:56.860
made with Nearline
last year, bringing it

00:29:56.860 --> 00:29:58.390
all the way to
instant access speed,

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:01.060
allowed us to ship a new storage
class that's called Coldline.

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:03.010
We've launched it last October.

00:30:03.010 --> 00:30:05.380
And Coldline is
best for cold data,

00:30:05.380 --> 00:30:07.840
specifically data that's
accessed less than once a year

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:09.310
on average.

00:30:09.310 --> 00:30:12.040
Similarly to Nearline, it has
all of the integration points

00:30:12.040 --> 00:30:13.690
it has exactly the same API.

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:17.380
But because it's built
for very cold data,

00:30:17.380 --> 00:30:20.020
customers usually use it
for archive or storing

00:30:20.020 --> 00:30:21.104
multimedia source files.

00:30:21.104 --> 00:30:23.020
So if you don't expect
to transcode your data,

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:25.480
that often you still want to
have your multimedia sources

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:27.680
somewhere and customers
choose Coldline

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:30.100
for that scenario and
also disaster recovery.

00:30:30.100 --> 00:30:32.590
And that's a very important
point with disaster recovery.

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:34.930
In case something happens
to your core data set,

00:30:34.930 --> 00:30:37.660
you want to be able to pull
the data out very quickly.

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:40.510
And usually customers cannot
use state media for this or any

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:42.280
sort of media that
has latency in it.

00:30:42.280 --> 00:30:44.530
And with Coldline you
have instant access speed

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:46.900
so you can start accessing
the data right away.

00:30:46.900 --> 00:30:48.654
And more than that,
because Coldline

00:30:48.654 --> 00:30:50.820
has exactly the same API
as any other storage class,

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:53.230
you can start serving
directly out of Coldline,

00:30:53.230 --> 00:30:55.390
or using the data that
your storing in Coldline

00:30:55.390 --> 00:30:57.670
for processing right
away without any delay,

00:30:57.670 --> 00:31:00.010
because API is exactly the same.

00:31:00.010 --> 00:31:01.901
And customers find
it much more simpler

00:31:01.901 --> 00:31:04.150
than any other solutions in
the market that they have.

00:31:04.150 --> 00:31:06.910
They just switch the
traffic to different bucket,

00:31:06.910 --> 00:31:08.380
and they start using it.

00:31:08.380 --> 00:31:10.600
In terms of price,
we charge $0.07

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:12.490
per gigabyte month for Coldline.

00:31:12.490 --> 00:31:15.074
And we also charge $0.05
per gigabyte on data access.

00:31:15.074 --> 00:31:17.740
And that works out that, if your
data is accessed less than once

00:31:17.740 --> 00:31:20.410
a year, Coldline becomes the
cheapest storage class for you

00:31:20.410 --> 00:31:21.670
to use.

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:24.070
We also provide availability
SLA for Coldline.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:25.705
We provide two lines
of availability

00:31:25.705 --> 00:31:28.080
SLA, exactly the
same as Nearline.

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:31.750
And we also have minimal
storage duration for Coldline

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:35.180
at 90 days.

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:37.720
So let me show you a
little bit about that API

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:38.596
and that performance.

00:31:38.596 --> 00:31:40.136
So people sometimes
don't believe me,

00:31:40.136 --> 00:31:41.814
do you really have
exactly the same API?

00:31:41.814 --> 00:31:42.980
Well, it's exactly the same.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:46.955
Here's an example of doing
GET or HTTP PUT with Coldline.

00:31:46.955 --> 00:31:48.580
And it's exactly the
same API as you've

00:31:48.580 --> 00:31:50.740
seen a few slides back with
Multi-regional storage.

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:52.073
You provide name of your bucket.

00:31:52.073 --> 00:31:53.600
In my case it was Coldline.

00:31:53.600 --> 00:31:56.650
I was the one to claim Coldline
as the name of my bucket.

00:31:56.650 --> 00:31:59.547
You provide your domain
name and then your file.

00:31:59.547 --> 00:32:00.130
And that's it.

00:32:00.130 --> 00:32:02.410
It's exactly the same
API as anywhere else.

00:32:02.410 --> 00:32:04.630
In terms of performance,
here's an example

00:32:04.630 --> 00:32:07.270
of reading one megabyte
out of Coldline.

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:09.910
So we have a utility
that's called gsutil,

00:32:09.910 --> 00:32:11.500
Google Storage
Utility, and then run

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:15.100
perfdiag with that utility and
downloaded one megabyte file

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:16.150
from Coldline.

00:32:16.150 --> 00:32:19.150
And as you can see here,
it's between 150 and 200

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:21.700
milliseconds to download
one megabyte file,

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:23.170
which is quite impressive.

00:32:23.170 --> 00:32:26.020
Many customers cannot achieve
this with their other solutions

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:26.660
for hot data.

00:32:26.660 --> 00:32:29.170
And we achieved this with
Coldline, less than 200

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:32.590
millisecond latency to pull
out one meg file out of coldest

00:32:32.590 --> 00:32:35.060
tier that we have.

00:32:35.060 --> 00:32:38.360
So we see a lot of
customers tiering that data

00:32:38.360 --> 00:32:39.890
from hot storage
to cold storage.

00:32:39.890 --> 00:32:42.860
But they can also tier
their storage from on-prem,

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:45.300
all the way to cloud, and
all the way to Coldline.

