the data within a
core data center,

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:55.550
or whether they're
running it in the cloud.

00:34:55.550 --> 00:34:58.760
And now, what makes
a cloud interesting

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:02.202
is customers look at Cloud
both from a data mobility

00:35:02.202 --> 00:35:04.160
perspective and from a
flexibility perspective,

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:09.230
but also looking for ways to
be able to offer lower cost

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:13.280
storage for some of their warm
or hot and potentially even

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:14.870
cold data, all right?

00:35:14.870 --> 00:35:18.130
So from an EMC perspective
and from a Dell perspective,

00:35:18.130 --> 00:35:19.550
we've got our own solutions.

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:22.550
We've got a product called
Elastic Cloud storage, which

00:35:22.550 --> 00:35:25.700
allows customers to
move data between Isilon

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:27.560
and then on-prem storage.

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:29.690
Now, what makes on-prem
storage interesting

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:31.310
is it gives
customers the ability

00:35:31.310 --> 00:35:37.280
to access the data within
the same protocol timeouts,

00:35:37.280 --> 00:35:39.950
within the same expected
reliability and response

00:35:39.950 --> 00:35:41.880
times from that cluster.

00:35:41.880 --> 00:35:45.584
Now, what that means is, when
we started looking at, OK,

00:35:45.584 --> 00:35:47.750
we've got an on-prem solution
in the form of Isilon.

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:51.290
We've got an object platform,
which is also on-prem.

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:53.840
Customers are looking
for off-prem solutions.

00:35:53.840 --> 00:35:56.290
Customers are looking for
public cloud solutions.

00:35:56.290 --> 00:35:59.007
And so we started to look
at a number of cloud targets

00:35:59.007 --> 00:35:59.840
that we can support.

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:03.050
And a key element to supporting
different cloud targets

00:36:03.050 --> 00:36:05.030
is, can we have a
cloud target that

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:07.760
enables customers to get
the dollar per terabyte

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:09.410
that they need
for their storage?

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:13.130
And also, can they get
the predictable SLAs

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:17.150
and the predictable latencies
in terms of access to that data.

00:36:17.150 --> 00:36:19.925
And that brings us to
partnering with Google,

00:36:19.925 --> 00:36:21.800
obviously as a very
important partner for us,

00:36:21.800 --> 00:36:26.570
is enabling customers to start
to tier all of their cold data

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:29.660
from the Isilon onto a
Google Cloud Storage target.

00:36:29.660 --> 00:36:32.180
So customers today
using Isilon can

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:35.990
put their performance data,
their high throughput data,

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:38.960
on a local on-prem
Isilon cluster.

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:42.440
But as the data starts to
age and the data gets cool,

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:45.470
you can start to move it off to
a Google Cloud Storage target

00:36:45.470 --> 00:36:47.937
and still be able to access
it within the same namespace

00:36:47.937 --> 00:36:49.520
that they would with
the data on-prem.

00:36:52.300 --> 00:36:54.080
What that gives
customers is essentially

00:36:54.080 --> 00:36:55.340
a seamless experience, right?

00:36:55.340 --> 00:36:57.740
Whether the data is
on-prem, whether the data is

00:36:57.740 --> 00:37:00.020
on a different tier of
storage within the cluster,

00:37:00.020 --> 00:37:02.630
or whether the data get steered
off to a Google Cloud Storage

00:37:02.630 --> 00:37:05.270
target, you get
the same experience

00:37:05.270 --> 00:37:06.620
from a cluster perspective.

00:37:06.620 --> 00:37:08.570
You have the same
security credentials.

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:10.580
You have the same
enterprise features.

00:37:10.580 --> 00:37:12.170
It's largely
agnostic and largely

00:37:12.170 --> 00:37:14.930
transparent to the customer,
whether the data is on-prem

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:16.520
or whether that's in the Cloud.

00:37:16.520 --> 00:37:19.160
And that's important for a lot
of the enterprise customers--

00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:21.830
and I'm sure a lot of people
can relate to that as well--

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:24.140
is your end customers
and end users

00:37:24.140 --> 00:37:25.700
essentially are
going to say, here's

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:27.910
what I need from a
storage perspective,

00:37:27.910 --> 00:37:31.760
here's the expected SLAs, and
here's the expected performance

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:33.815
characteristics that I
expect from my storage.

00:37:33.815 --> 00:37:35.450
You, storage vendor,
go figure out

00:37:35.450 --> 00:37:37.130
how do you provide that to me.

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:39.070
And for us being able
to do that with a vendor

00:37:39.070 --> 00:37:40.970
like Google enables
customers to make

00:37:40.970 --> 00:37:43.662
that choice of whether they
want to keep that data on-prem

00:37:43.662 --> 00:37:46.120
or in the cloud, but essentially
keep it seamless for them.

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:55.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:55.720 --> 00:37:58.320
KIRILL TROPIN: So we've talked
about tiering data from on-prem

00:37:58.320 --> 00:38:00.844
to Coldline and to Nearline.

00:38:00.844 --> 00:38:02.260
Turns out that
many customers want

00:38:02.260 --> 00:38:05.260
to tier the data from hot
storage to cold storage

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:06.200
as well.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:07.660
So before we go
into tiering, I'd

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:09.534
like to remind you all
of the storage classes

00:38:09.534 --> 00:38:11.690
that we have in Google
Cloud Storage today.

00:38:11.690 --> 00:38:12.770
So we have four of them.

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:15.490
It's Multi-regional, Regional,
Nearline, and Coldline.

00:38:15.490 --> 00:38:17.950
So two left ones,
Multi-regional and Regional,

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:19.390
are hot storage classes.

00:38:19.390 --> 00:38:22.750
Multi-regional is geo redundant
across two or more regions

00:38:22.750 --> 00:38:24.250
that are 100 miles apart.

00:38:24.250 --> 00:38:26.680
And then Regional is
designed for hot data

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:28.630
stored within a single region.

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:31.180
And Multi-regional is typically
used for content storage

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:31.820
and delivery.

00:38:31.820 --> 00:38:33.820
And Regional is typically
for big data analytics

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:35.170
within a given region.

00:38:35.170 --> 00:38:37.640
Then we also have two storage
classes for cool data.

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:39.850
That's Nearline for
infrequently accessed data.

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:41.594
And we also have
Coldline storage class.

00:38:41.594 --> 00:38:43.510
That's for data that's
accessed less than once

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:46.650
a year that's typically
archive and very cold data.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:49.600
And across all of them we have
the same durability, 11 9's.

00:38:49.600 --> 00:38:51.300
We have the same
instant access speed.

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:54.640
We have the same API
across all of them.

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:57.100
One interesting feature
that we shipped last year

00:38:57.100 --> 00:38:59.800
was Object Level storage
class and data lifecycle

00:38:59.800 --> 00:39:01.510
across those storage classes.

00:39:01.510 --> 00:39:04.690
So we see many customers
wanting to use multiple storage

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:07.060
classes at the same time
and age out their data

00:39:07.060 --> 00:39:09.490
from hot storage to cold
storage over time and pay

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:10.510
less over time.

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:12.850
And we've seen customers copy
the data from hot storage

00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:15.130
to cold storage and when
they decide they need to use

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:16.214
or they will copy it back.

00:39:16.214 --> 00:39:18.088
And there was quite
inefficient for customers

00:39:18.088 --> 00:39:20.050
so we wanted to make it
very simple for them.

00:39:20.050 --> 00:39:23.900
And simplify that they don't
need to worry about that.

00:39:23.900 --> 00:39:25.757
So we've created Per
Object storage class.

00:39:25.757 --> 00:39:28.090
With Per Object storage class,
you can keep your bucket,

00:39:28.090 --> 00:39:30.560
you can keep your
name of your objects,

00:39:30.560 --> 00:39:33.010
and you just change settings
on a given object and say,

00:39:33.010 --> 00:39:35.590
now this object is Nearline or
now this object is Coldline.

00:39:35.590 --> 00:39:37.060
But everything stayed the same.

00:39:37.060 --> 00:39:40.300
So objects stayed in place,
is the same object name,

00:39:40.300 --> 00:39:42.770
the same bucket name,
the same API call.

00:39:42.770 --> 00:39:44.980
So if your application
was using that object,

00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:46.960
it wouldn't notice any
change and everything

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:49.270
will keep working as intended.

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:52.120
And we also ship data lifecycle
across storage classes.

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:54.670
You can create a simple
policy that will say,

00:39:54.670 --> 00:39:57.940
if my objects are older than 30
days, or some number of days,

00:39:57.940 --> 00:39:59.380
move them to Nearline.

00:39:59.380 --> 00:40:01.570
As we see, a lot of
customers use their data

00:40:01.570 --> 00:40:03.490
very frequently
within first 30 days,

00:40:03.490 --> 00:40:07.060
but then they no longer use
the data after those 30 days.

00:40:07.060 --> 00:40:09.484
And typically, they would
move the data to Nearline.

00:40:09.484 --> 00:40:10.900
And after a longer
period of time,

00:40:10.900 --> 00:40:12.900
they'll move it to Coldline
as they don't expect

00:40:12.900 --> 00:40:14.930
to use the data much longer.

00:40:14.930 --> 00:40:16.720
So what's cool about
this project storage

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:19.780
class and our consistent API
across those storage classes,

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:21.786
once data ends up
in Coldline, you

00:40:21.786 --> 00:40:24.160
don't need to worry about
restoring it back or copying it

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:24.790
somewhere.

00:40:24.790 --> 00:40:27.130
You can keep accessing
against the same API,

00:40:27.130 --> 00:40:28.810
against the same object name.

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:30.910
And that provides a
very simple solution

00:40:30.910 --> 00:40:35.250
for our customers that your data
between hot and cold storage.

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:37.859
I like to compare us
to S3, because there

00:40:37.859 --> 00:40:39.650
are quite a few areas
where we are winning.

00:40:39.650 --> 00:40:43.340
So starting from the far right
is Multi-regional storage.

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:46.730
And our competitor does not
have really direct competition

00:40:46.730 --> 00:40:47.730
in that area.

00:40:47.730 --> 00:40:50.360
So you could create
the S3 standard

00:40:50.360 --> 00:40:52.020
with cross-regional application.

00:40:52.020 --> 00:40:54.260
And you would need to pay
for two regional copies.

00:40:54.260 --> 00:40:55.801
And you would need
to pay for network

00:40:55.801 --> 00:40:58.190
to replicate across those
two regional copies.

00:40:58.190 --> 00:41:01.589
And that adds up to much
higher number of like 4.2 just

00:41:01.589 --> 00:41:02.130
for storage--

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:03.800
4.2 cents per gigabyte month--

00:41:03.800 --> 00:41:06.230
plus network to replicate
across the regions.

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:08.420
And with us, we have a
much simpler solution

00:41:08.420 --> 00:41:09.830
with Multi-regional storage.

00:41:09.830 --> 00:41:11.840
You just create one bucket, and
we'll take care of everything

00:41:11.840 --> 00:41:12.245
for you.

00:41:12.245 --> 00:41:13.710
You don't need to
pay for second copy,

00:41:13.710 --> 00:41:15.800
you don't need to pay for
network to replicate it,

00:41:15.800 --> 00:41:17.550
you don't need to worry
about failing over

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:19.980
between those two regions
if one of them goes down.

00:41:19.980 --> 00:41:22.320
We'll take care of
all of that for you.

00:41:22.320 --> 00:41:24.020
And because of probably
that simply API,

00:41:24.020 --> 00:41:26.720
all of our storage classes
have exactly the same API

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:28.180
and exactly the
same performance.

00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:30.263
You don't need to configure
anything extra outside

00:41:30.263 --> 00:41:32.070
of those storage classes.

00:41:32.070 --> 00:41:34.610
So then if we move to
Regional storage, that's

00:41:34.610 --> 00:41:36.242
for hot data access
within the region,

00:41:36.242 --> 00:41:37.700
we have a quite
comparable product.

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:40.010
It's GCS Regional
versus S3 standard.

00:41:40.010 --> 00:41:43.370
And we are slightly cheaper
than our competition

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:44.450
around the world.

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:46.520
And then going to
infrequent accessed data,

00:41:46.520 --> 00:41:49.740
we have GCS Nearline, which is
at $0.01 per gigabyte month.

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:51.950
And our competition
charges 1.25 cents

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:55.160
per gigabyte month-- so
again, slightly cheaper there.

00:41:55.160 --> 00:41:57.830
And then going to cold
storage, so we have product

00:41:57.830 --> 00:41:59.180
that's called GCS Coldline.

00:41:59.180 --> 00:42:00.860
We launched it five months ago.

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:02.510
And with Coldline,
we have the same API

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:04.510
and the same latency
characteristics

00:42:04.510 --> 00:42:05.912
as any other storage classes.

00:42:05.912 --> 00:42:07.370
And that means
customers don't need

00:42:07.370 --> 00:42:08.840
to worry about
integrating with it

00:42:08.840 --> 00:42:11.630
or treating it differently
like anything else.

00:42:11.630 --> 00:42:15.860
And there is no direct
competition in that space.

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:17.800
So when customers
think about storage

00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:20.952
and comparing it to
Amazon S3, usually they

00:42:20.952 --> 00:42:23.410
think it's apples-to-apples
comparison between Google Cloud

00:42:23.410 --> 00:42:25.624
Storage, or GCS, and S3.

00:42:25.624 --> 00:42:27.040
But turns out,
with a S3, you need

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:29.110
to add a lot of different
layers to compare

00:42:29.110 --> 00:42:30.640
to what GCS provides.

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:32.890
So if you want to use
a really cold storage,

00:42:32.890 --> 00:42:34.850
you need to add Glacier,
you need to figure out

00:42:34.850 --> 00:42:35.740
to integrate with it.

00:42:35.740 --> 00:42:37.480
You need to restore
data out of Glacier

00:42:37.480 --> 00:42:39.469
before it becomes usable.

00:42:39.469 --> 00:42:41.260
If you want to have
Multi-regional storage,

00:42:41.260 --> 00:42:42.676
or geo redundant
storage, you need

00:42:42.676 --> 00:42:44.950
to use CRR, which is
an extra API for you

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:47.830
can figure those regions
and failover between them.

00:42:47.830 --> 00:42:51.160
If you want to use
high throughput network

00:42:51.160 --> 00:42:54.019
or if you want to get high
throughput for your data,

00:42:54.019 --> 00:42:55.560
you need to use
Accelerated Transfer.

00:42:55.560 --> 00:42:56.650
And they charge extra for that.

00:42:56.650 --> 00:42:58.316
With us, we don't
charge anything extra.

00:42:58.316 --> 00:43:00.820
All of your data is
accelerated out-of-the box,

00:43:00.820 --> 00:43:04.160
because we use fantastic Google
network to transfer your data.

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:06.370
And then, if you
want to use CDN,

00:43:06.370 --> 00:43:08.950
you need to include the
CloudFront with Amazon.

00:43:08.950 --> 00:43:10.995
With us, edge caching
is part of the product.

00:43:10.995 --> 00:43:12.370
And as you've seen
from Artur, we

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:14.060
have fantastic latency
because of that,

00:43:14.060 --> 00:43:16.060
because we have so many
different tiers of cache

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:17.650
and it's part of
the core product

00:43:17.650 --> 00:43:19.000
that you don't need
to pay anything extra.

00:43:19.000 --> 00:43:21.330
You don't need to configure
anything on top of that.

00:43:21.330 --> 00:43:25.760
It is part of our simple
Google Cloud Storage.

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:30.210
To summarize, we have
a large portfolio

00:43:30.210 --> 00:43:31.960
that covers many
different use cases.

00:43:31.960 --> 00:43:33.600
So we have Multi-regional
storage class

00:43:33.600 --> 00:43:35.640
that's perfect for content
storage and delivery.

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:37.181
We have Regional
storage class that's

00:43:37.181 --> 00:43:38.950
perfect for big data analytics.

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:40.950
We have Nearline for
infrequently accessed data.

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:42.780
And we have Coldline
for cold data.

00:43:42.780 --> 00:43:44.460
All of the storage
classes are united

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:47.770
by the same API, the same online
latency, the same network,

00:43:47.770 --> 00:43:50.640
the same tiering that
we have at Google.

00:43:50.640 --> 00:43:53.065
Now, we have lifecycle
across those stories classes

00:43:53.065 --> 00:43:55.440
that you don't need to worry
about configuring or copying

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:57.570
any data between
those storage classes

00:43:57.570 --> 00:43:59.070
and then changing your API.

00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:01.720
Everything is very
smooth for you right now.

00:44:01.720 --> 00:44:03.930
And we also have lots of
different partnerships

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:05.850
that help you
integrate and help you

00:44:05.850 --> 00:44:08.651
use Google Cloud Storage in any
scenario that you might have.

00:44:08.651 --> 00:44:10.650
So you can learn more
about Google Cloud Storage

00:44:10.650 --> 00:44:12.580
at cloud.google.com/storage.

00:44:12.580 --> 00:44:14.580
And you might have heard
that this morning we've

00:44:14.580 --> 00:44:18.060
announced a much larger
free trial with Google Cloud

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:18.600
Platform.

00:44:18.600 --> 00:44:21.180
And we also announced Free Tier,
which includes Google Cloud

00:44:21.180 --> 00:44:21.720
Storage now.

00:44:21.720 --> 00:44:25.350
So everyone has access to free
five gigabytes in the United

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:26.340
States.

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:27.630
And it doesn't expire.

00:44:27.630 --> 00:44:28.630
There is no limit on it.

00:44:28.630 --> 00:44:29.640
So it's Free Tier.

00:44:29.640 --> 00:44:32.100
Feel free to use it and feel
free to try out Google Cloud

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:33.510
Storage.

00:44:33.510 --> 00:44:35.820
So we have quite some time left.

00:44:35.820 --> 00:44:37.800
So maybe a few
questions, if there any

00:44:37.800 --> 00:44:39.909
questions in the audience.

00:44:39.909 --> 00:44:41.200
So we had three guest speakers.

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:43.770
So all of us can
answer your questions.

00:44:43.770 --> 00:44:45.940
And I was asked, if
you have any questions,

00:44:45.940 --> 00:44:47.670
please stand up and
walk to the mic.

00:44:47.670 --> 00:44:49.740
It's because we are
recording, so that we'll

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:52.114
hear a question in
the recording as well.

00:44:52.114 --> 00:44:54.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:54.600 --> 00:44:55.110
Thank you.

